/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Two springs of the same free length placed one inside the other are compressed by the same amount: they act in parallel.
   - P₁ and P₂ are the loads on the outer and inner spring in N; k₁ and k₂ their rates in N/mm; the set's rate is k₁ + k₂.
   - The two springs are wound in opposite hands, one right-hand and one left-hand, so that a coil of one cannot slip between the coils of the other and jam the set.
   - The standard design assumes both springs are of the same material, have the same free length, the same solid length, the same spring index C and the same maximum stress.
   - d₁ and d₂ are the wire diameters of the outer and inner springs.
   - The radial clearance between the springs is usually taken as half the difference of the wire diameters, c = (d₁ − d₂)/2.
   - A spring in a valve train or clutch is loaded between Pmin and Pmax again and again.
   - Pm = (Pmax + Pmin)/2
   - Pm is the mean load; the alternating load is Pa = (Pmax − Pmin)/2.
   - Indian textbooks such as Bhandari apply only the direct-shear factor Ks = 1 + 0.5/C to the mean stress and the full Wahl factor K to the alternating stress, because the curvature effect behaves like a stress concentration, which matters onl
*/
