/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The longest leaf, the master leaf, is rolled into an eye at each end for the shackle pins.
   - A semi-elliptic spring of eye-to-eye span 2L₁ with central load 2W acts as two cantilevers of length L, each loaded by W at its eye.
   - The clamped centre does not bend, so the effective length is 2L = 2L₁ − l, where l is the width of the centre band (about two-thirds of the U-bolt spacing when U-bolts are used).
   - With nf full-length leaves (including the master leaf), ng graduated leaves, n = nf + ng, leaf width b and thickness t, the full-length leaves are stressed 50 % more than the graduated ones:
   - σf = 18WL/[bt²(3nf + 2ng)]
   - σf is the stress in the full-length leaves; the graduated leaves carry σg = 12WL/[bt²(3nf + 2ng)].
   - δ = 12WL³/[Ebt³(3nf + 2ng)]
   - δ is the deflection at the eye relative to the centre, in mm; E the Young's modulus, about 207 000 MPa for spring steel.
   - To stress all leaves equally, the full-length leaves are given a slightly larger radius of curvature than the graduated leaves.
   - σ is the equal stress in every leaf after nipping.
*/
